diff --git a/Servex/ext/remote/doc/tunnel.md b/Servex/ext/remote/doc/tunnel.md
index f4ae7127b..5039fe321 100644
--- a/Servex/ext/remote/doc/tunnel.md
+++ b/Servex/ext/remote/doc/tunnel.md
@@ -41,7 +41,11 @@ These routes still pass on purpose, because they are the features: `/api/item` (
 
 **Running it.** `node Server/Tunnel.mjs up` starts both gateways in named mode (the keeper does this). `node Server/SiteGateway.mjs` runs the site gateway alone. Test: `node Server/SiteGateway.test.mjs`.
 
-**No stale files on a phone (2026-11-01).** A phone kept an old `Sheet.js` for days: Chrome served it from its HTTP cache without asking, because an older reply had no `Cache-Control` (a heuristic cache; the dev server has sent `no-cache` on static files since 2026-10-05, but that copy was older). The gateway now gives every reply without one `no-cache`. On a page load from a browser whose `lew42_cache_v` cookie is not `SiteGateway.CACHE_V`, it also sends `Clear-Site-Data: "cache"` and sets the cookie, so each browser is cleared once. On that load it drops the browser's `If-None-Match` and `If-Modified-Since`, so the site sends the page whole: the clear never rides a 304, and a 304 is what a phone that already has the page would get. Chrome may hold that one page load for a moment while it clears; a slow first load after a `CACHE_V` bump is expected. It clears the HTTP cache only; cookies, the site key and storage stay. To clear every browser again, bump `CACHE_V`. The clear works in the tab that loads the page fresh (a new tab, or the next load). A tab already open keeps its in-memory copies for one more load. Only the gateway does this; localhost is untouched. Proof: `node Server/SiteGateway.cache.test.mjs`. It seeds a stale nested import, shows that it sticks, then shows that the next page load gets the new one.
+**No stale files on a phone (2026-11-02).** A phone kept an old `Sheet.js` and `Recite.js` for days: Chrome served them from its HTTP cache without asking (Android Chrome restoring a discarded tab prefers the cache, whatever `no-cache` says). So through this gateway, HTML, JS, CSS and JSON (and `.jsonl`) go out as `Cache-Control: no-store`: a browser has nothing to go stale, and every load asks the site. `no-store` is chosen by content type or by file name (`SiteGateway.NO_STORE_TYPE`, `NO_STORE_PATH`); every other reply without a `Cache-Control` (images, fonts) gets `no-cache`, and an event stream is left alone. For copies already stuck on a phone, a page load from a browser whose `lew42_cache_v` cookie is not `SiteGateway.CACHE_V` (now `"2"`) also gets `Clear-Site-Data: "cache"` and the cookie is set, so each browser is cleared once. On that load the gateway drops `If-None-Match` and `If-Modified-Since`, so the clear never rides a 304. Chrome may hold that one load for a moment while it clears. It clears the HTTP cache only; cookies, the site key and storage stay. To clear every browser again, bump `CACHE_V`. The clear works in the tab that loads the page fresh (a new tab or the next load); a tab already open keeps its in-memory copies for one more load. Only the gateway does this; localhost is untouched. Proof: `node Server/SiteGateway.cache.test.mjs`.
+
+*Not built, on purpose:* a deploy-version `CACHE_V` from git and an inline self-heal script (fetch `/__version`, re-fetch the page's modules, reload once). With `no-store` there is nothing to heal. Both exist in commit `70770dd3f` on branch `worktree/cache-version` if a gap shows up.
+
+*Cost of no-store (measured 2026-11-02, a 360x740 phone viewport loading `/framework/` through a gateway on this PC, no tunnel in the path):* 873 requests, about 0.5 MB, loaded in 0.4 s cold and 0.25 s warm, idle at 1.3 s. The warm load makes the same 872 requests as the cold one (before, they were conditional requests answered 304, so the request count is not new, only the bytes). Through the tunnel each of those is a round trip: see *Measured through the tunnel* below. If that is slow, the next step is versioned URLs: an import map that maps `/framework/` to `/v/<git hash>/framework/`, served `immutable`, with the HTML staying `no-store`.
 
 **Later: a real login.** Once Access is enabled (Zero Trust → team name → Free plan) and the token has **Access: Apps and Policies → Edit**, put an Access application with the owner's email in front of `monorepo.lew42.com`; the key cookie can then go.
 
